The first topic I found interesting was the Gulf of Execution and the Gulf of Evaluation on pages 38–39. Norman explains that the Gulf of Execution happens when someone knows what they want to accomplish but has trouble figuring out what action to take. The Gulf of Evaluation happens afterward, when the person has trouble understanding whether their action actually worked or what the device is doing. I agree with this idea because I have experienced it with websites, apps, and even everyday electronics. Sometimes I know exactly what I want to do, but the controls are not clear enough to tell me how to do it. Other times I press something and receive so little feedback that I am unsure whether anything happened. Norman points out that good feedback and a clear conceptual model can make the Gulf of Evaluation smaller (p. 39). This made me think more about how a design should communicate with the user instead of expecting the user to figure everything out by trial and error.
Another topic that interested me was Norman's discussion of the visceral, behavioral, and reflective levels of processing on pages 49–55. The visceral level involves our immediate and automatic reactions, while the behavioral level relates more to performing actions and expectations. The reflective level involves conscious thought, decision-making, and how we think about an experience afterward (p. 50). What I found especially interesting is that these levels are connected to emotion. A poorly designed product can cause frustration or helplessness, while a well-designed one can create enjoyment, satisfaction, and a sense of control (p. 55). I agree with Norman here because design is not only about whether something technically works. The way a product makes someone feel while using it can affect whether they want to continue using it at all. This section helped me see that good design needs to consider both how people think and how they emotionally respond to their experience.
